Follow-Up Criteria
Use these rules to decide who deserves your time.

Prioritise
Choose A when there is a clear and genuine reason to continue the conversation.
Choose B when there may be a reason, but you need more context first.
Choose C when there is no clear reason to follow up right now.

Good reasons
You discussed a specific business or professional problem.
The person asked a question or requested a resource.
You can offer a relevant introduction or useful information.
Your work and the person's current work are meaningfully connected.
You agreed to speak again.

Avoid
Contacting every connection automatically.
Using a job title as the only reason to contact someone.
Pretending to remember a conversation that did not happen.
Making promises that you cannot support.
Continuing after someone asks not to be contacted.
